# MCP servers (stub)

This directory is a **scaffolded stub** for plugins that need external APIs.
Per [Model Context Protocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/), an MCP server
exposes tools/resources to the agent harness. **Only keep this directory when
your plugin actually calls an external API** — otherwise delete it.

## What's here

- `{{PLUGIN_PREFIX}}-server.mjs` — a minimal stdio MCP server stub with one
  example tool (`echo`). Replace the tool with your real capability.

## When to use

| Situation | Action |
|-----------|--------|
| Plugin needs an external API (weather, search, git host, LLM, …) | Keep this dir; implement the real tool(s) |
| Plugin is purely local (files, skills, orchestration) | Delete `mcp-servers/` — no external dependency |

## Wiring

1. Install the SDK inside the plugin (do not add it to the stub — the stub has
   **zero runtime dependencies**):
   ```sh
   npm install @modelcontextprotocol/sdk
   ```
2. Implement tools in `{{PLUGIN_PREFIX}}-server.mjs` (replace the `echo` example).
3. Register the server with the harness:
   - Claude Code: `.mcp.json` with `{ "mcpServers": { "{{PLUGIN_PREFIX}}": { "command": "node", "args": ["mcp-servers/{{PLUGIN_PREFIX}}-server.mjs"] } } }`
   - Other harnesses: follow their MCP config (pi / opencode register MCP servers
     through their own config files).
4. Add contract tests for each tool in `tests/` (TDD loop, per pf-build).

## Contract rules

- The stub must stay dependency-free so `npm install` on the generated plugin is
  optional — external SDKs are added only when the user opts into an API.
- Every tool needs an acceptance test; a tool with no test is not complete.
- Never embed secrets in `mcp-servers/` — read them from the environment.
